summer doesn't officially start for  another week but we are already  expecting record  breaking heat this afternoon we are  keeping an eye on those temperatures we  do have team coverage  starting with of course chief milo just  ted florendo but we also have bianca  holman and she has some warnings from  the national weather service  now so staying out of the sun and  staying hydrated is something we're used  to  living here in las vegas and in southern  nevada but experiencing these hot  temperatures for  several days well that's something we're  not used to we were able to check in  with the national weather service and  they say that there's nothing typical  about our temperatures this week there  are records that may be broken with  temperatures running 10 to 15 degrees  above normal  in 2013 a heat wave in southern nevada  had 30 fatalities and more than 350 heat  related injuries  this heat is serious and officials want  everyone to stay safe through this  extended heat wave  it's actually very unusual and one of  the questions we get a lot is  why do we worry about the heat it's the  summer in the desert that's true it does  get warmer at this time but not to this  fast and doesn't last this long  and we're talking about record  temperatures being broken not just in  one site we're talking about  over 40 different locations that might  be seeing record-breaking temperatures  and not for one day  possibly five six or even seven days so  this is  quite a bit uncommon this is not typical  desert southwest heat you might think  hitting the lake will cool you off but  that could be a dangerous situation as  well the water may be cooler but the  land around the lake is hotter than in  the city  you can easily experience hypothermia  heat stroke or heat exhaustion  and of course another reminder when it  comes to our vehicles they say put a  something  very important maybe like your purse or  your wallet in the back seat so that you  don't forget your child  or your pet heat related deaths are very  very serious  and they can happen in a matter of  minutes bianca holman  8 news now
 

